course: dictionary
lesson: 5137
title: Invite: asking, and making trouble more likely
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Invite has a few meanings, and the context tells you which one. «Lily invited girls from both streets to her birthday» means she asked them to come to her party. The clue is the birthday, because a party is an event you go to.
explain: Invite can also mean asking for something politely, as in «We invite questions». To invite trouble means to make something bad more likely. Ask yourself what the sentence is about: a party, a question, or something going wrong.
explain: «When friends want to leave someone out, invite them in; it takes courage to be kind when others aren't.» Here invite means asking someone to join in. The word "in" is a clue too: you bring the person inside the group.
explain: Invite can also be a short, informal noun, an invite, which means an invitation. You might say "Got an invite?" to a friend, but use invitation in your writing at school.
fact: Invite is a verb: invite, invites, invited, inviting.
fact: «Invite» means to ask someone to come to a party, a meal, your home or an event.
fact: «Invite» can also mean to ask for something politely, such as «We invite questions».
fact: «Invite trouble» means to make something bad more likely.
fact: Phrases to know: invite over and invite round (UK) mean asking someone to come to your home, and invite to means asking someone to a particular event.
exercise: «Lily invited girls from both streets to her birthday, and her mum had to buy twice as many party bags.» What does invited mean here? | *Asked them to come to the party | Told them to stop playing | Pushed them away from the house | Asked them to buy party bags
exercise: Which action in «Kindness is small and quick online» is an example of inviting someone who is left out? | *Inviting someone who always plays alone | Telling an adult about bullying | Reporting a post | Saying "nice one" to a friend
exercise: In safety lessons, what should you do with an invite from someone you met online? | Invite them to your home | *Do not accept it | Give them your address | Accept it straight away
test: What does «invite trouble» mean? | *To make something bad more likely | To say thank you for a present | To ask a friend round for tea | To run away quickly
test: «The hongi is a traditional Maori greeting in New Zealand, done when you are invited.» What does this tell us? | *It is a greeting done when someone has asked you to take part | It is a game played at school | It is a dance done only at night | It is a meal you cook yourself
exam: Which sentence uses invite to mean asking for something politely? | Lily invited girls to her birthday | *We invite questions at the end of the lesson | Do not invite trouble by hiding your phone | Her invite was printed on pink paper
